    "created_at": "2026-01-20T05:41:09Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-01-20T05:46:18Z",
    "body": "> If appropriately related in scope, I don't see the concern of adding into the Web apps working group.\n\nRight, but we literally just got the new Web Apps charter approved... we would need to recharter web apps again to add this, right? \n\n> Yes, there are several specifications there, but I'm not sure how putting them in another working group would improve the overall progress in the portfolio. If anything it would cause scheduling conflicts at things like TPAC.\n\nThat's actually fine... what we want is focused communities. \n\n> think the joint approach is quite good, given the scopes of the groups.\n> This should also minimize any companies need when considering participating\n> as most companies expected to participate in these have already engaged in\n> these working groups, and it seems the incremental evaluation of charter\n> updates is easier than joining new groups.\n\nI don't disagree... but that wouldn't change with a new group either (more specs eats more time at TPAC regardless, so it's just a tradeoff and one that can be managed... we manage fine with DAS and WebApps overlaps... we just meet on another day). \n\nConsider how effectively groups that are tightly scoped work  (e.g., FedID with digital credentials, Payments and the Payment Request API, and I'm sure @anssiko concurs with respect to WebML... small focused groups and communities work effectively!). \n\nI know those groups take a few months to set up. But this work is not going anywhere (it's been incubating for **12 years and 2 months**)... there spec is more or less done. Let's not pretend there's suddenly a huge hurry. \n\nWhat would be cool is if we had some kind of working group that allowed us to do APIs that have a security model that goes beyond the Web's current one: like, Mozilla is not going to expose this API to every website, IIUC. They are only exposing it on a *per requested site basis* through their web extensions mechanism. Grouping APIs like that somehow (and into a new working group) seems like potentially a good idea (to me). \n\n\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
